{"slides":[{"k":"title","h":"WHAT are we sourcing?","sub":"Session 1 live class · From request to category insight","note":"Open the call five minutes early and greet people by name. Say in one sentence what today is for: the videos gave the theory (the 11 steps, intake, TCO, Porter, preferencing, Kraljic, the Chessboard); today we test it on their own Sourcing Case projects and on Nordvia, and we disagree on purpose. Repeat the rule of the course aloud, because every debate today comes back to it: analyse the category and the power position first, choose the strategy second, pick the tool last. Check quickly in the chat that everyone has a Sourcing Case project; those without one choose from their contract expiries in the first break, with Nordvia P1 cleaning as the fallback."},{"k":"table","h":"Today's plan","t":[["Time","Block","How we work"],["09:00","Welcome, check-in, your Sourcing Case","Plenary, poll"],["09:20","1.1 S2C as a decision chain","Debate, pairs"],["09:55","1.2 Start right: intake and triage","Trios, poll"],["10:30","Break",""],["10:40","1.3 Know the category: spend, contracts, TCO","Open floor, own fact base"],["11:15","1.4 Know the market and how it sees us","Trios, poll"],["11:50","Break",""],["12:00","1.5 Power decides strategy","Agree / disagree, trio challenge"],["12:30","Case 1: the Nordvia pipeline; fieldwork","Breakout trios, then plenary"]],"note":"Clock times assume a 09:00 start; shift them if your class starts later. Point out that every module ends with the same rhythm: one provocation, one practice block in the workbook, a debrief and a three-line recap. Agree the ground rules in one sentence: cameras on, chat welcome at any time, and the breakout trios stay the same for the whole course because each trio will review its members' Sourcing Cases. Keep this slide under two minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Energiser: finish the sentence","q":"\"In our team, a sourcing event really starts when...\"","b":["Type it, but do not press Enter yet","Everyone sends on my count of three","Describe what happens, not what the policy says"],"min":6,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Count down so everybody sends at once and nobody copies. Read six or seven answers aloud. Expect things like 'when the business sends three quotes', 'when the contract has already expired', 'when Finance asks for a PO'. Laugh with the room, do not defend the process. Then ask one person whose answer described a late start: what could you still change at that moment? Usually only the price. Bridge: Nordvia's team of 9 runs about 60 events a year, mostly RFQs with three quotes, whatever the category. Today we find out why that is a problem and what to do before the RFQ."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s1-l1","note":"Give 30 seconds. Expect most votes on 'an expiring contract'; that is good news, because expiring contracts are predictable and make up most of a mature sourcing pipeline (our Group planning view puts them at 60 to 70%). If several people pick 'an urgency', ask one of them: was it really unforeseeable, or did nobody look at the end date? Anyone who chose 'I still need to choose one' gets a private message from you now: pick a contract expiring in 6 to 18 months, above about €250K over the term, with a business owner who will give an hour. Fallback: Nordvia P1 cleaning."},{"k":"section","n":"1.1","h":"S2C as a decision chain","sub":"Where is the value decided, and who decides it?","note":"35 minutes for this module. Do not re-lecture the 11 steps: the video covered them. The goal is that each person sees, with their own last three events, which steps they skip. Ask them to open Practice 1.1 in the workbook now; if they filled it before class, great, if not they do it in the next ten minutes."},{"k":"flow","h":"Steps 1 to 5 decide; steps 6 to 11 execute","s":["Need","Category","Market","Power","Strategy","Tool","Contract"],"p":"Steps 1 to 5 produce almost nothing a supplier sees. That is why they get skipped.","note":"Walk the chain left to right and ask at each box: who in your team decides this today, and is it written anywhere? Need, category, market and power are today's session; strategy is Session 2; tool is Session 3; contract and handover Session 4. Make the point that CIPS, van Weele and O'Brien all put the strategy choice after the insight work, and that O'Brien's Innovation stage only starts after Insight. Then give them ten minutes to complete Practice 1.1 individually (last three events, done, rushed or skipped for each step) before the debate. Remind them nobody else sees it unless they share."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Is three quotes good governance?","q":"\"Three quotes is good governance. It protects us and it is fair to suppliers.\"","b":["What does three quotes prove, and what does it not prove?","Which of your last three events skipped steps 1 to 5?","Name one category where three quotes is exactly right"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Ask for a show of hands or reactions, then call one person from each side. The defenders are partly right: three quotes is a fine tool for a well-specified, commoditised purchase with enough bidders. The problem is using it for everything. Push with Nordvia: the same RFQ for office cleaning, IT consulting and a sole-source Kelvar renewal. Ask: what does a three-quote RFQ achieve with a vendor that has no real competitor? Nothing, except a delay and a weaker negotiation. Then ask two or three people to share the step they marked 'skipped' most often in Practice 1.1. Typical pattern: need validation and market analysis are rushed or skipped; RFx and contract are always done. Write the most skipped step in the chat. Mention, labelled as a consultancy benchmark, that Hackett's 2024 'Digital World Class' group shows 23% shorter sourcing cycles: speed comes from knowing the category before the request, not from skipping analysis."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"1.1 in three lines","b":["S2C is a decision chain, not a document chain","Value is decided in steps 1 to 5, the ones we skip","The tool follows the strategy, never the reverse"],"note":"Read the three lines and ask one person to say which line their Practice 1.1 confirmed. Keep it to one minute. If the room is behind schedule, this is the slide to shorten. Transition: if steps 1 to 5 matter, step 1 matters most, because it decides whether we go to market at all."},{"k":"section","n":"1.2","h":"Start right: intake and triage","sub":"The cheapest saving is the purchase that does not happen","note":"35 minutes. Two messages to land: challenge the need before sourcing it, and ask the regulatory questions on day one, not at contract signature. Remind them of the six intake questions and the four triage questions from the video; the workbook has both in Practice 1.2."},{"k":"discuss","h":"What would you do? \"Just extend Telmo\"","q":"IT asks you to extend the Telmo network contract (€4.9M a year, Nordvia P4) for another 12 months. It has already been rolled over twice. \"No time for a tender.\"","b":["Which intake question do you ask first?","What does the triage say? Claims systems use this service","What do you say to IT, in one sentence?"],"min":10,"format":"Breakout trios","note":"Send trios to breakout rooms for six minutes, then take three answers in plenary. Expected points: the first question is ownership and timing (question 6: nobody owned the renewal, which is why it rolled over twice); then value and term, because two extensions mean real money without competition. Triage: Telmo provides services used by claims systems, so it is very likely an ICT service under DORA (register of information, Art. 30 clauses) and the team must ask whether it supports a critical or important function; that is a documented assessment with Risk and Information Security, not Procurement's call alone. Note that traditional analogue telephony is outside DORA's ICT definition, but connectivity used by claims systems is a different story. A good sentence to IT: 'We can agree a short, documented bridge, but only with a dated plan to market and the DORA checks started now.' Pitfall: trios that say 'run an RFQ' straight away. Ask them: on which specification, and is the supplier market even known?"},{"k":"poll","poll":"s1-l2","note":"Give 30 seconds. The best answer is 'Possibly: Risk and Compliance must assess and document it'. Claims handling is among the examples of critical or important functions in EIOPA's governance guidance, and scanning claims documents may support it; the more substantial and ongoing the service, the more likely it is outsourcing (EIOPA Q&A 1283 says a one-off expert appointment may not necessarily amount to outsourcing). The determination is the insurer's own documented assessment. If anyone picks 'not a question for Procurement', push back: Procurement does not decide it alone, but it must ask on day one, because a yes changes the provider examination, the written agreement (access, audit, termination rights), the supervisor notification and the lead time. Also note the personal data angle (GDPR, data processing agreement). If anyone mentions the EIOPA cloud guidelines, correct it kindly: they were withdrawn with effect from 17 January 2025; for ICT the reference is DORA."},{"k":"debrief","h":"Debrief: your own intake and triage","b":["Did your need survive the challenge, or did the scope shrink?","Annual value or total contract value: which did you write?","Which triage box did you tick 'possible yes', and who confirms it?","What is your latest date to start, counted back from the notice period?"],"note":"Give twelve minutes for Practice 1.2 in the workbook first (intake and triage for their own project), then debrief for eight. Ask for one example of a need that shrank on challenge, such as two overlapping studies or a framework that already exists at Group level. Check total contract value: many people quote the annual figure only. Use the Nordvia example: cleaning at €2.4M a year over 3 + 1 + 1 years is about €12M total value, which changes the approval level. Ask who ticked an ICT or outsourcing box; each of those becomes a fieldwork action to confirm with Risk, Compliance or Information Security. Finally, ask someone to read their 'latest date to start': if it is already in the past, that is the most useful finding of the morning."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"1.2 in three lines","b":["Challenge the need before you source it","Think in total contract value, counted from the notice date","Ask the regulatory questions at intake, document the answers with Risk"],"note":"One minute. Mention that good practice is to build the triage questions into the Coupa intake form so requests route to Risk, Compliance or Information Security automatically; which form and routing their entity uses is a configuration choice to check in their own Coupa tenant. Then announce the break."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break: 10 minutes","p":"Back at 10:40. Think about it: which number in your own category would surprise your business owner?","note":"Put a countdown in the chat. The question plants the next module: the fact base. Anyone who still has no Sourcing Case project should pick one now; message them individually. Restart on time."},{"k":"section","n":"1.3","h":"Know the category","sub":"Spend, contracts, demand drivers, total cost of ownership","note":"35 minutes. O'Brien calls this Insight: no strategy before the facts. The video showed the spend cube, the contract inventory, demand drivers and Ellram's three TCO phases. Here we use Nordvia P2 to show how a fact base already tells the story, then they build their own."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Is the problem a missing tender?","q":"Nordvia IT consulting (P2): €6.1M a year, 12 firms, 38 call-offs, no framework. The top three firms take 61%. Senior day rates range from 820 to 1,450 euros for the same profile.","b":["What does the fact base tell you, before any market contact?","Would a tender fix it? Which kind?","Who in IT will resist, and why?"],"min":10,"format":"Open floor","note":"Let people react to the numbers first. The key insight: the problem is not a missing tender, it is a missing structure (rate card, a panel of preferred suppliers, call-off rules). A one-off tender would fix one price for one project and leave the other 37 call-offs untouched. The rate spread for the same profile is the strongest data point; ask what explains it (relationships, urgency, no benchmark). Resistance: project managers who like 'their' consultants and fast direct calls; Session 2 deals with call-off rules that keep speed. Group lens to mention: about 90 supplier groups work for two or more entities, around €50M managed in some 360 separate local relationships, and only about 2% of spend is covered by a Group contract today. Ask: is your own category bought separately by several entities? Do not go into panel design yet; that is Session 2."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Cheaper price, cheaper deal?","q":"\"If the new cleaning bid is 5% cheaper on price, Nordvia saves money.\"","b":["Which pre-transaction costs come with a new supplier?","Which post-transaction costs does a cheap bid risk?","What is in your own TCO baseline beyond price?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Take votes, then ask the 'agree' side to defend. Walk the illustrative TCO baseline for Nordvia P1 (clearly say the figures beyond the contract price are illustrative, not case data): contract price €2.40M, extras ordered outside the contract about €0.15M, contract management about €0.04M, quality failures about €0.03M, mobilisation if the supplier changes about €0.05M a year spread over the term. A bid that doubles complaints or needs a costly mobilisation may not be cheaper. Labour is the dominant cost in cleaning, so a much lower price usually means fewer hours or wages below the floor. Then give ten minutes for Practice 1.3 (fact base and TCO baseline for their own project) and ask two people for the one insight from their fact base that will shape the strategy. Pitfall: TCO lines with no source; insist they mark estimates as estimates."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"1.3 in three lines","b":["The fact base often tells the story before the market does","The notice period, not the end date, is the real deadline","Price is one line of the TCO, not the TCO"],"note":"One minute. Ask one person to say which data item they could not find in Coupa; that becomes a Fieldwork 1 action (24 months of spend, contracts with notice, auto-renewal and indexation, Group frameworks). Transition: spend analysis shows our side; now we look at theirs."},{"k":"section","n":"1.4","h":"Know the market and how it sees us","sub":"Spend analysis shows our side. Market analysis shows theirs.","note":"35 minutes. The video covered Porter, PESTLE, cost drivers, capacity, the RFI and Steele & Court's supplier preferencing. The live focus is preferencing, because it is the half of the power picture buyers usually forget, and it must rest on evidence."},{"k":"discuss","h":"How does Kelvar see Nordvia?","q":"Kelvar (P3): €11.2M a year, a 9% uplift at the last renewal, no usage data, an auto-renewal clause. Core, Development, Exploitable or Nuisance?","b":["What is your evidence, not your feeling?","What evidence would prove you wrong?","What would make Nordvia more attractive to Kelvar?"],"min":10,"format":"Breakout trios","note":"Six minutes in trios, then each trio gives its quadrant and one piece of evidence. The most defensible reading is Exploitable: high value to the supplier, but a customer that never challenges uplifts gives the supplier little reason to invest. Evidence for: the uplift history, auto-renewal accepted, no usage data on our side. Counter-evidence would be investment, roadmap influence or flexibility; ask if anyone knows of any. On attractiveness: growth, reference value, ease of doing business and payment behaviour; bundling Group volume can raise it. Do not let trios argue about the 'right' answer; reward the trio with the clearest evidence. Mention DORA's market question for ICT: concentration risk, how many alternatives exist, how dependent are we and the Group. Pitfall: 'they like us, we have a good relationship' is not evidence."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s1-l3","note":"Ask them to vote for their own Sourcing Case incumbent, then give ten minutes for Practice 1.4 (market snapshot and preferencing position, with evidence). Look at the distribution: many rooms vote Core, because buyers like to think suppliers value them. Ask one Core voter for evidence (priority service, investment, flexibility, roadmap access) and one Nuisance or Exploitable voter what that means for the strategy. 'No evidence yet' is an honest and useful answer: it becomes a fieldwork question."},{"k":"debrief","h":"Debrief: market snapshot and the RFI question","b":["How many credible suppliers can really deliver your project?","Which boxes did you tick 'assumed'?","Do you need an RFI, and for what: discovery, pre-qualification, or an alternative to the incumbent?","Who would challenge your preferencing position, and with what?"],"note":"Take three or four examples. On the RFI: it is useful when the market is new or changing, to pre-qualify on regulatory points (certifications, data location, subcontracting chain, financial standing) or to build a credible alternative to an incumbent such as Kelvar. It is a bad idea when the market is well known, as free consulting, or when you cannot offer a next step. A two-to-three-week response window is common practice, not a rule. Ask: who has ever sent an RFI and never answered the suppliers? That damages our attractiveness for the next event."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"1.4 in three lines","b":["Know how many suppliers can really deliver, and at what cost","Ask how the supplier sees us, and prove it with evidence","Use an RFI to learn or to build an alternative, not as a ritual"],"note":"One minute, then the break. Ask one person to read the line that changed their view of their own project; often it is the preferencing line, because they had never asked how the supplier sees them. Remind them that every 'assumed' box in Practice 1.4 is a question for Fieldwork 1. Plant the next question: in your project, who holds more power, you or the supplier?"},{"k":"statement","h":"Break: 10 minutes","p":"Back at 12:00. Who holds more power in your project: Nordvia's buyer or the supplier?","note":"Countdown in the chat. During the break, check the workbook submissions for Practices 1.3 and 1.4 and pick one interesting Sourcing Case to use as an example in 1.5, with the participant's permission. Restart on time."},{"k":"section","n":"1.5","h":"Power decides strategy","sub":"Three lenses, one direction","note":"30 minutes. Kraljic for the buyer's view, Steele & Court for the supplier's view, and the Purchasing Chessboard to turn the balance of power into levers. Today we only name the direction; the route to market is Session 2."},{"k":"cards","h":"The Chessboard: four basic strategies","c":[["Manage spend","Low demand power, low supply power: demand management, bundling"],["Leverage competition","High demand power, low supply power: tendering, target pricing"],["Seek joint advantage","High demand power, high supply power: value and cost partnership"],["Change the nature of demand","Low demand power, high supply power: re-specification, innovation, risk management"]],"note":"Use this as a quick reminder, two minutes. Ask: where does each power come from? Demand power grows with volume, bundling across areas and entities, freedom to change the specification and a credible ability to switch. Supply power grows with concentration, proprietary technology, switching cost and scarce capacity. Then give eight minutes for Practice 1.5: score their own project on both Kraljic and the Chessboard and write the direction in one sentence. Each trio then challenges one member's positioning in the debate that follows."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Strategic means partner?","q":"\"A strategic supplier deserves a partnership.\"","b":["Strategic for us, Exploitable for them: partner or danger zone?","What does 'change the nature of demand' mean for Kelvar?","Trio challenge: is your own positioning based on evidence?"],"min":12,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Most people agree at first. Turn it around with the combination table from the video: strategic plus Core preferencing does justify a partnership; strategic plus Exploitable or Nuisance is the danger zone, where the strategy must first raise our attractiveness or reduce our dependency. For Kelvar that means usage data and shelfware review, re-specification, a credible alternative (the case data mentions two credible platforms, with migration of 18 to 24 months), and timing to the renewal, never an auction. Then send trios for five minutes: each challenges one member's Practice 1.5 positioning. Positions are judgements, not calculations: accept disagreement if the evidence is explicit. Pitfall: people putting every IT purchase in 'strategic'; ask how many alternatives really exist."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"1.5 in three lines","b":["Kraljic shows our view, preferencing shows theirs","The Chessboard turns the power balance into levers","Name the direction first; the route comes in Session 2"],"note":"One minute. Give the Session 2 preview in one sentence, without teaching it: leverage services like cleaning and security usually lead to one supplier by tender; consulting, IT services and agencies to a panel of preferred suppliers; a strategic or bottleneck incumbent like Kelvar to a single-source negotiation. Then move straight to the case."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Case 1: position Nordvia's pipeline","min":15,"where":"Breakout trios, Case tab in your workbook","b":["Eight projects, P1 to P8: Kraljic, Chessboard, how the supplier sees us","Flag the regulatory triage for each","Name the direction in a few words","Which two projects first, and why?"],"note":"Send the fixed trios to breakout rooms. One person types the team answer in the Case tab. The pipeline is in the workbook: cleaning, IT consulting, Kelvar, Telmo, office supplies and print, security and reception, creative agencies, Northbyte. Advise them to start with the four big IT projects, because that is where the disagreement is. Visit each room for a minute and ask: what evidence did you use for supply power? Warn them they present in two minutes per trio, three points maximum."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s1-l4","note":"Run the poll as soon as trios return, before the presentations. Defensible answers: Kelvar, because of value (€11.2M a year) and timing: a 9-month notice before the June 2027 renewal means the real deadline comes long before the end date, and building a BATNA takes time. Northbyte, because it is the largest (€19.4M), supports a critical function, is in the DORA register and has no tested exit plan, which is a regulatory gap whatever the expiry. Telmo, because it is already running on extensions and touches claims systems. If votes go to cleaning or office supplies, ask whether they chose the easiest or the most important project; the link to course 1 is the same: invest where value is high. Accept any choice argued with value, risk and timing."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Debrief: where would three quotes have failed?","q":"Which Nordvia projects would \"RFQ with three quotes\" have served badly, and why?","b":["Each trio: two minutes, three points","Peer challenge: one question per trio","Where did your trio disagree?"],"min":12,"format":"Trio presentations","note":"After the presentations, compare with one possible reading (do not present it as the only answer). P1 cleaning, P6 security, P5 office supplies and print: leverage; P5 close to non-critical, to be channelled through a catalogue. P2 consulting and P7 creative agencies: leverage moving to strategic, managed through a panel. P3 Kelvar: bottleneck or strategic with high supply power, probably Exploitable: change the nature of demand. P8 Northbyte: strategic, DORA critical function, Solvency II outsourcing to check; the exit plan is the gap. P4 Telmo: leverage or bottleneck, DORA ICT service; the rollover itself is the risk. Three quotes fails worst for Kelvar (no real competition), Northbyte (regulatory and exit), P2 and P7 (the problem is structure, not one price). It is roughly right only for parts of P5. Protect presenters from pile-ons: one question per trio."},{"k":"bullets","h":"Fieldwork 1, before Session 2","b":["45-minute need-validation meeting with your business owner","Complete the data: 24 months of spend, contracts, notice, Group frameworks","Confirm every 'possible yes' in the triage with Risk, Compliance or Information Security","Update your fact base, TCO and positioning; share a one-page profile with your trio"],"note":"Point to the meeting guide in the Fieldwork tab: eleven questions, from the outcome they need to what happens if the new arrangement is late. Stress the rule: listen for positions ('we need supplier X') and ask for the interest behind them; do not propose a route to market yet, that is Session 2. Ask each person to put the meeting date in their calendar before leaving the call and to type the date in the chat."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"Session 1 in five lines","b":["S2C is a decision chain: steps 1 to 5 decide the value","Challenge the need and triage the regulation at intake","Build the fact base and the TCO before talking to suppliers","Know the market and how the supplier sees us, with evidence","Power decides the direction; the tool comes last"],"note":"Ask everyone to type in the chat the one step they will stop skipping. Read a few aloud. Close on the bridge to Session 2: next time the question is WHICH route to market, single supplier by tender, panel, or negotiation, and their positioning from today is the input. End on time and stay five minutes for anyone unsure about their Sourcing Case project."}],"polls":[{"id":"s1-l1","q":"How did your Sourcing Case project reach you?","options":["An expiring contract","A new business need","A proactive initiative (panel, consolidation, Group bundling)","An urgency","I still need to choose one"]},{"id":"s1-l2","q":"Nordvia moves claims document scanning from an in-house team to a BPO provider. What is it?","options":["Outsourcing of a critical or important function: full Solvency II path","Possibly: Risk and Compliance must assess and document it","Not outsourcing: a normal service purchase","Not a question for Procurement"]},{"id":"s1-l3","q":"How does the incumbent of your Sourcing Case most likely see your account?","options":["Core","Development","Exploitable","Nuisance","No evidence yet"]},{"id":"s1-l4","q":"Which Nordvia project should the team start first?","options":["P3 Kelvar licences","P8 Northbyte claims IT","P4 Telmo network","P2 IT consulting","P7 Creative agencies","P1 Office cleaning"]}]}